 This problem is about solving a system of linear equations. We can represent the values of triangles and circles as variables: Δ for triangles and Θ for circles. (\bigcirc is not a standard LaTeX command for circles, so I will use Δ and Θ instead)
We can translate the problem into the following system of linear equations:
\begin{align*}
5Δ + 3Θ &= 21\\
3Δ + 5Θ &= 16
\end{align*}
We can solve this system of equations using the elimination method or substitution method. Here, I will use the elimination method.
First, we will multiply the first equation by 5 and the second equation by 3:
\begin{align*}
25Δ + 15Θ &= 105\\
9Δ + 15Θ &= 48
\end{align*}
Next, we will subtract the second equation from the first equation:
\begin{align*}
(25Δ + 15Θ) - (9Δ + 15Θ) &= 105 - 48\\
16Δ &= 57\\
Δ &= \frac{57}{16}
\end{align*}
Now that we have the value of Δ, we can substitute it back into one of the original equations to solve for Θ. I will use the first equation:
\begin{align*}
5 \left(\frac{57}{16}\right) + 3Θ &= 21\\
\frac{285}{16} + 3Θ &= 21\\
3Θ &= 21 - \frac{285}{16}\\
3Θ &= \frac{336}{16} - \frac{285}{16}\\
3Θ &= \frac{51}{16}\\
Θ &= \frac{17}{16}
\end{align*}
Finally, we are asked to find the sum of two circles, which is 2Θ:
\begin{align*}
2Θ &= 2 \left(\frac{17}{16}\right)\\
2Θ &= \frac{34}{16}\\
2Θ &= \frac{17}{8}
\end{align*}
The sum of two circles is \(\frac{17}{8}\). The final answer is:
\boxed{\frac{17}{8}}

